blocks.body:
The \textbf{line of best fit} (or \emph{least-squares regression line}) is the single straight line that passes as close as possible to all of the points at once. You are never asked to compute it by hand. You are asked to \emph{use} it:
\begin{itemize}\setlength{\itemsep}{2pt}
\item read a predicted $y$ off the line for a given $x$;
\item read the $x$ that produces a given predicted $y$;
\item find its slope from two points the line passes through;
\item say what its slope and its $y$-intercept mean in context.
\end{itemize}
Everything the line says is a \emph{prediction}, never a fact about an individual subject. The word ``predicted'' belongs in every correct interpretation.

In an interpretation question the wrong choices are not random. The favourite distractor is the correct sentence with the two numbers traded: for $\hat{y}=24x+150$, the key says ``the predicted number of members increases by $24$ each month,'' and the trap says ``$\dots$ increases by $150$ each month.'' Two more to watch for:
\begin{itemize}\setlength{\itemsep}{2pt}
\item \textbf{Variables swapped}: ``for each additional member, $24$ more months,'' which reverses what is predicted from what.
\item \textbf{Units swapped}: if $y$ is measured in thousands of people or in dollars, a slope of $1.6$ may mean $1600$ people, and a slope of $0.19$ dollars may mean $19$ cents.
\end{itemize}
And when a question asks for the \emph{residual}, the predicted value is always sitting there among the choices.

